Scope: internal/services/mcpsvc/snapshot/snapshot_test.go (manifest .swarm/manifests/X1.2.files, single file). Verified in a scratch copy at /tmp/claude-1000/.../scratchpad/x1-check/budget2 (repo copied with `cp -a`, kept .git, worked entirely in the copy; only real-tree write is this verdict file).

TECHNICAL CONSTRUCTION OF THE NEW TEST — SOUND
1. TestSnapshotter_FailsWhenSourceMissing is an honest rename of the old
   TestSnapshotter_FailsWhenSourceUnreadable (git diff confirms body
   unchanged, only the name and doc reference moved).
2. EISDIR-as-proxy-for-unreadable does NOT short-circuit on a different
   branch than a genuine unreadable regular file. Empirically confirmed
   (small probe program + read of os/file.go semantics): os.Open succeeds on
   a directory; the failure happens inside the subsequent Read(), which is
   exactly the call os.ReadFile(src) makes inside Ensure. Ensure calls
   `data, err := os.ReadFile(src)` as a single unit — whether the underlying
   failure is EACCES-on-Open (chmod 0000) or EISDIR-on-Read (directory), the
   err != nil branch taken in Ensure is identical, and no different earlier
   branch exists. The "short-circuits earlier" attack does not hold.
3. No-leak assertion is a real assertion, not decorative: mutation-tested
   two ways in the sandbox and reverted after each:
   a. Swallow the read error and proceed with empty data → new test fails
      with "expected an error for an unreadable source file" (confirms it
      checks err, not just byte content).
   b. Reorder Ensure to touch/write the destination file before reading the
      source, so a read failure now leaves a stray .bak behind → new test
      fails with "snapshot dir should still be empty after a failed read,
      found: [... .bak]" (confirms the leak check is live against the real
      snapshotDir, not a stale/empty-by-construction assertion).
   Both mutations reverted; diff against pre-mutation snapshot.go confirmed
   clean (`diff` exit 0) before re-running the full suite.
4. Error-naming assertion verified against the real produced message:
   "cannot snapshot whatif.json before writing: read
   .../whatif.json: is a directory" — contains "whatif.json" as claimed.
5. Baseline green: `go build ./...` clean; `go test -count=1 ./...` full
   suite green (all packages ok, including
   budget2/internal/services/mcpsvc/snapshot); `go test -count=1 -v
   ./internal/services/mcpsvc/snapshot/...` green, both old and new
   subtests present and passing. Toolchain go1.26.6 confirmed
   (`go version`).
6. Uncommitted files outside the manifest (dataloader/loader.go,
   retirement/overrides.go, retirement/settings*.go,
   dataloader/alias_stable_id_test.go, swarm/t7-coverage.sh, ledger.tsv,
   X2-X5 manifests, X5 tier3 dir) are sibling-task X2-X5 artifacts per the
   dispatch note — not attributed to X1, not touched, not counted against
   this verdict.

DEFECT FOUND — fabricated citation used to justify the new test
The new test's doc comment claims it "covers the case Ensure's doc comment
gives as the reason it reads bytes instead of hard-linking: if the store is
encrypted in a directory this MCP doesn't know is encrypted, a blind copy of
unreadable ciphertext must still fail loudly rather than silently
'succeeding' with a useless backup (docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md
§3)." I read that document directly (in the scratch copy). §3
("Data-directory resolution") is about two unrelated bugs: (a) cmd/whatif-mcp
detecting the `.encrypted` marker in the wrong directory, causing a JSON
parse error instead of a "storage is locked" message, and (b)
BUDGET_DATA_DIR not being honored. Neither sub-point says or implies anything
about os.ReadFile aborting on an unreadable source, and neither is about the
snapshot package at all. This citation does not support the claim it is
attached to — it is fabricated provenance.

Worse, the rationale is also internally contradicted by the very
snapshot.go doc comment it claims to be summarizing. Ensure's own comment
(lines 47-50) says: "It copies the file's raw bytes with os.ReadFile rather
than hard-linking it... When the store is encrypted the bytes are
ciphertext, which is what a hand-restore back into that same store needs."
That is: an encrypted source file is NOT an unreadable-source case at all —
os.ReadFile happily reads ciphertext bytes; that's the documented reason
Ensure copies raw bytes instead of decrypting. The new test's comment
conflates "encrypted" with "unreadable," inventing a scenario (encrypted →
read failure) that contradicts the code comment sitting eleven lines above
it in the same package.

The technical test itself (directory-as-source, EISDIR) is a legitimate,
mutation-verified proxy for "source cannot be read," and would have been
sound on its own steam citing Ensure's actual missing-file-must-abort
guarantee (lines 52-54: "A missing source file is an error, and the caller
must abort its write on it... every caller snapshots BEFORE writing
precisely so that a failure here prevents an unrecoverable change") extended
naturally to any read failure. It did not need the encryption tie-in at all.
But as committed, the comment attaches a false documentary citation and a
factually wrong rationale to an otherwise-correct test. A maintainer trusting
this comment would misunderstand both what docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-
09.md actually says and what Ensure actually guarantees about encrypted
stores. Given this task's own framing turns on the rename being done
"honestly," a fabricated/contradicted citation in the new test's justification
is exactly the kind of defect this lane exists to catch, and defaults to FAIL
on the ambiguity of whether "honest" test provenance was maintained.

VERDICT: FAIL — technical assertions in the new test are correct and
mutation-verified, but its doc comment cites docs/whatif-mcp-followups-
2026-08-09.md §3 for a claim that document does not make, and states a
rationale (encrypted-store bytes are "unreadable") that directly contradicts
Ensure's own neighboring doc comment. Recommend: strip the §3 citation and
the encryption tie-in, and ground the comment in Ensure's actual
missing-source-must-abort guarantee instead.
